	{
		"name": "execute_sql",
		"title": "Execute SQL atomically",
		"description": "Use SQL as the universal interface to Lix. Lix SQL is a Postgres dialect subset. execute_sql runs any ordered atomic SQL batch against the branch selected by context and is always treated as potentially destructive. File and directory creation, reads, edits, deletes, path changes, and entity operations are SQL. Do not look for or invent typed file or entity tools. Lix SQL does not support DDL statements.\n\nCore tables:\n- `lix_file` — Current files in the active branch. Columns: `id, path, directory_id, name, content (binary)`.\n- `lix_directory` — Current directories in the active branch. Columns: `id, path, parent_id, name`.\n- `lix_branch` — Lix branches and their current commits. Columns: `id, name, hidden, commit_id`.\n- `lix_change` — Global immutable change ledger; use it for activity and audit. Columns: `id, entity_pk, schema_key, file_id, snapshot_content, metadata, created_at, origin_key`.\n\nPath rules: `lix_file.path` and `lix_directory.path` are canonical absolute paths and neither ends in `/` (except the root path `/`). `lix_file.directory_id` references `lix_directory.id`; `lix_directory.parent_id` references `lix_directory.id`. Use those IDs for hierarchy queries.\n\n`lix_file.content` is stored as bytes. For UTF-8 / text files, pass `$1` (path) and `$2` (file body) as `kind: \"text\"` and write with `INSERT INTO lix_file (path, content) VALUES ($1, CAST($2 AS BYTEA))`. Never use `CAST(... AS BLOB)`; if a SQL cast is needed, use `BYTEA`. Actual binaries use `kind: \"blob\"` without CAST.\n\nTo create a file, use `INSERT INTO lix_file (path, content) VALUES ($1, CAST($2 AS BYTEA)) ON CONFLICT (path) DO UPDATE SET content = excluded.content`; to read it, use `SELECT path, CAST(content AS TEXT) AS content FROM lix_file WHERE path = $1`; to rename it, use `UPDATE lix_file SET path = $2 WHERE path = $1`. Create directories with `INSERT INTO lix_directory (path) VALUES ($1) ON CONFLICT (path) DO NOTHING`.\n\nLix supports Postgres `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (cols) DO UPDATE` / `DO NOTHING` with `excluded.col` on files (`lix_file` on `path` or `id`) and schema-driven views such as `lix_key_value`. Example: `INSERT INTO lix_key_value (key, value) VALUES ($1, $2) ON CONFLICT (key) DO UPDATE SET value = excluded.value`. This is how you create-or-update without a probe SELECT. Name the conflict columns; `ON CONSTRAINT`, inference without columns, and `DO UPDATE WHERE` are not supported.\n\nFor a mutation, inspect the relevant rows first with query_sql, execute the mutation, and verify it with a focused SELECT. Put related mutations and their final verification SELECT in one execute_sql call so the change is atomic and the returned final result is its readback. Use rowsAffected plus returned rows to report what changed; never infer success from prose alone.\n\nA successful execute_sql mutation is already saved; Lix does not require a checkpoint for durability. Unlike Git, skipping create_checkpoint keeps the files; you just do not have a named restore point, and `lix_working_diff()` keeps accumulating from the last checkpoint. create_checkpoint is optional later, when a session or task is complete and someone may want this exact state; do not call it after every write. List checkpoints with `SELECT` on `lix_checkpoint` or `lix_history('lix_checkpoint')`.\n\nSQL tables always use the branch selected by the current Lix session. They do not expose branch-routing columns or `_by_branch` variants.\n\nEntity tables are lix-dynamic: do not assume a fixed entity-table list or column shape. Before guessing a dynamic table or column name, discover its table name through information_schema, then inspect only that table:\n\n```sql\nSELECT table_name, table_type\nFROM information_schema.tables\nWHERE table_schema = 'public'\nORDER BY table_name;\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT column_name, data_type, is_nullable, ordinal_position\nFROM information_schema.columns\nWHERE table_schema = 'public'\n  AND table_name = $1\nORDER BY ordinal_position;\n```\n\nSystem `lixcol_*` metadata is intentionally omitted from the core guide. History table functions are read-only revision streams, not current-state snapshot tables. Call `lix_history('lix_file')`, `lix_history('lix_directory')`, or `lix_history('<schema>')` to start at the active branch head. For an explicit anchor commit, pass its id as the second function argument, for example `lix_history('lix_file', $1)` or `lix_history('<schema>', $1)`. The relation name must be a non-null text literal, not a SQL parameter. Constrain the result by stable identity: `id = $n` for files/directories or the schema's declared primary-key columns for entities, resolved from the current table. Read a current item's `lixcol_change_id` from its current table, not an historical row; use `lixcol_depth` / `lixcol_observed_commit_id` for prior revisions.\n\nStatements may be read-only or mutating. If any statement fails, the complete batch is rolled back.",
		"inputSchema": {
			"$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
			"type": "object",
			"properties": {
				"context": {
					"type": "string",
					"minLength": 1,
					"description": "Opaque Lix context returned by open_lix or switch_branch"
				},
				"statements": {
					"minItems": 1,
					"type": "array",
					"items": {
						"type": "object",
						"properties": {
							"sql": {
								"type": "string",
								"minLength": 1
							},
							"params": {
								"type": "array",
								"items": {
									"anyOf": [
										{
											"type": "object",
											"properties": {
												"kind": {
													"type": "string",
													"const": "null"
												},
												"value": {
													"type": "null"
												}
											},
											"required": ["kind"],
											"additionalProperties": false
										},
										{
											"type": "object",
											"properties": {
												"kind": {
													"type": "string",
													"const": "bool"
												},
												"value": {
													"type": "boolean"
												}
											},
											"required": ["kind", "value"],
											"additionalProperties": false
										},
										{
											"type": "object",
											"properties": {
												"kind": {
													"type": "string",
													"const": "int"
												},
												"value": {
													"type": "integer",
													"minimum": -9007199254740991,
													"maximum": 9007199254740991
												}
											},
											"required": ["kind", "value"],
											"additionalProperties": false
										},
										{
											"type": "object",
											"properties": {
												"kind": {
													"type": "string",
													"const": "float"
												},
												"value": {
													"type": "number"
												}
											},
											"required": ["kind", "value"],
											"additionalProperties": false
										},
										{
											"type": "object",
											"properties": {
												"kind": {
													"type": "string",
													"const": "text"
												},
												"value": {
													"type": "string"
												}
											},
											"required": ["kind", "value"],
											"additionalProperties": false
										},
										{
											"type": "object",
											"properties": {
												"kind": {
													"type": "string",
													"const": "blob"
												},
												"base64": {
													"type": "string"
												}
											},
											"required": ["kind", "base64"],
											"additionalProperties": false
										},
										{
											"type": "object",
											"properties": {
												"kind": {
													"type": "string",
													"const": "json"
												},
												"value": {}
											},
											"required": ["kind", "value"],
											"additionalProperties": false
										}
									]
								}
							}
						},
						"required": ["sql"],
						"additionalProperties": false
					}
